UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a27C$Correctly recognizes charge separation but understates it as polar covalent sharing and assigns the partial negative charge to the less electronegative atom$a27C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a27D$Reverses the direction of electron transfer, sending the electron to the less electronegative cesium instead of to fluorine$a27D$ FROM q;

-- Q4 [Ionic Character & Bond Type · medium · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans D] (g28)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Ionic Character & Bond Type',
    $qt28$A chemist comparing four binary bonds is given the Pauling electronegativities \( \text{Ca} = 1.0 \), \( \text{Al} = 1.6 \), \( \text{C} = 2.6 \), \( \text{S} = 2.6 \), \( \text{Cl} = 3.2 \), and \( \text{O} = 3.4 \). Using only the rule that a larger electronegativity difference corresponds to greater ionic character, which of the bonds \( \text{Ca-O} \), \( \text{Al-Cl} \), \( \text{S-O} \), and \( \text{C-Cl} \) is most likely the most ionic?$qt28$,
    $op28$[{"label":"A","text":"\\( \\text{C-Cl} \\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\( \\text{S-O} \\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\( \\text{Al-Cl} \\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\( \\text{Ca-O} \\)"}]$op28$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex28$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because the ionic character of a bond rises with the absolute electronegativity difference \( |\Delta\text{EN}| \) between the two bonded atoms: the larger the difference, the more unequally the bonding electrons are shared and the closer the bond sits to the ionic end of the bonding continuum. Computing each difference from the given values gives \( \text{Ca-O} = |1.0 - 3.4| = 2.4 \), \( \text{Al-Cl} = |1.6 - 3.2| = 1.6 \), \( \text{S-O} = |2.6 - 3.4| = 0.8 \), and \( \text{C-Cl} = |2.6 - 3.2| = 0.6 \). The \( \text{Ca-O} \) bond has the largest \( |\Delta\text{EN}| \), so it is the most ionic, pairing a low-electronegativity metal with a highly electronegative nonmetal. (Choice A) \( \text{C-Cl} \) has the smallest difference of the set, \( |2.6 - 3.2| = 0.6 \), so it is the most nearly covalent of the four rather than the most ionic. (Choice B) \( \text{S-O} \) has \( |\Delta\text{EN}| = 0.8 \), only slightly larger than \( \text{C-Cl} \) and far below \( \text{Ca-O} \); both atoms are electronegative nonmetals, so the difference stays small. (Choice C) \( \text{Al-Cl} \) has the second-largest difference, \( |1.6 - 3.2| = 1.6 \), which makes it strongly polar but still smaller than the \( 2.4 \) gap in \( \text{Ca-O} \), so it is not the most ionic. Skill 2$ex28$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc28$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc28$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a28A$selects the bond with the smallest electronegativity difference as most ionic, inverting the larger-difference-more-ionic rule$a28A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a28B$judges ionic character from an absolute electronegativity value rather than the difference between the two bonded atoms$a28B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a28C$correctly identifies a strongly polar bond with a large difference but stops at the second-largest difference instead of the maximum$a28C$ FROM q;

-- Q5 [Ionic Character & Bond Type · medium · Skill 1 · general-chemistry · ans B] (g29)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Ionic Character & Bond Type',
    $qt29$In gaseous \(\text{HCl}\), the \(\text{H-Cl}\) bond has an electronegativity difference of about \(0.9\), placing it in the polar covalent range. How is the bonding electron pair distributed between the two atoms?$qt29$,
    $op29$[{"label":"A","text":"It is transferred completely to chlorine, leaving a full \\(+1\\) charge on hydrogen and a full \\(-1\\) charge on chlorine."},{"label":"B","text":"It is shared but drawn closer to chlorine, giving chlorine a partial negative charge \\((\\delta^-)\\) and hydrogen a partial positive charge \\((\\delta^+)\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"It is shared exactly equally between the two atoms, so neither atom carries any net or partial charge."},{"label":"D","text":"It is shared but drawn closer to hydrogen, giving hydrogen a partial negative charge \\((\\delta^-)\\) and chlorine a partial positive charge \\((\\delta^+)\\)."}]$op29$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex29$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because an intermediate electronegativity difference produces a polar covalent bond in which the shared pair is pulled toward the more electronegative atom, generating partial charges rather than full ion charges. With \(\Delta\text{EN} \approx 0.9\), the \(\text{H-Cl}\) bond lies in the polar covalent regime (roughly \(0.4\) to \(1.7\)), so the electrons are not transferred outright but remain shared while spending more time near chlorine; this unequal sharing leaves chlorine with a partial negative charge \((\delta^-)\) and hydrogen with a partial positive charge \((\delta^+)\), which is what is meant by partial ionic character. (Choice A) Complete transfer of the electron pair to give full \(+1\) and \(-1\) charges describes a purely ionic bond, which would require a much larger \(\Delta\text{EN}\) (generally above about \(1.7\)); at \(\Delta\text{EN} \approx 0.9\) the electrons are still shared, so only partial charges develop. (Choice C) Exactly equal sharing with no charges occurs only in a nonpolar covalent bond between atoms of equal electronegativity \((\Delta\text{EN} \approx 0)\); a difference of \(0.9\) guarantees unequal sharing and therefore partial charges. (Choice D) This places the partial negative charge on the wrong atom: electron density is drawn toward the more electronegative atom, and chlorine is more electronegative than hydrogen, so chlorine bears the \(\delta^-\) and hydrogen the \(\delta^+\). Skill 1$ex29$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc29$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc29$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a29A$polar covalent bond mistaken for full electron transfer producing complete ion charges$a29A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a29C$correctly identifies sharing but assumes it is equal, ignoring the nonzero electronegativity difference$a29C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a29D$partial charges assigned with polarity reversed, placing delta-minus on the less electronegative atom$a29D$ FROM q;

-- Q6 [Ionic Character & Bond Type · easy · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans A] (g30)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Ionic Character & Bond Type',
    $qt30$In carbon disulfide \(\text{CS}_2\) (connectivity S=C=S), each carbon-sulfur bond joins atoms whose electronegativities are \(2.55\) for carbon and \(2.58\) for sulfur. Classify each \(\text{C}{-}\text{S}\) bond by type.$qt30$,
    $op30$[{"label":"A","text":"Nonpolar covalent, because \\(\\Delta\\text{EN}\\approx 0.03\\) is essentially zero"},{"label":"B","text":"Polar covalent, because the two atoms are different elements"},{"label":"C","text":"Ionic, because sulfur is more electronegative than carbon"},{"label":"D","text":"Polar covalent, because the bond is part of a \\(\\text{C}{=}\\text{S}\\) double bond"}]$op30$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex30$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because the electronegativity difference \(\Delta\text{EN}=2.58-2.55=0.03\) is far below the \(\approx 0.4\) threshold, so the bonding electrons are shared essentially equally and the bond is nonpolar covalent. Bond character is determined by \(\Delta\text{EN}\): values near \(0\) (roughly \(0.0\)-\(0.4\)) give nonpolar covalent bonds, intermediate values give polar covalent bonds, and large values (roughly \(\geq 1.7\)) give predominantly ionic bonds; with \(\Delta\text{EN}\approx 0.03\), \(\text{C}{-}\text{S}\) sits at the nonpolar covalent end despite carbon and sulfur being different elements. (Choice B) Two different elements do not automatically make a polar bond; polarity depends on the magnitude of \(\Delta\text{EN}\), which here is negligibly small. (Choice C) An \(\text{ionic}\) classification requires a large \(\Delta\text{EN}\) (roughly \(\geq 1.7\)); a difference of \(0.03\) is nowhere near that range, so no appreciable charge transfer occurs. (Choice D) Bond order (single versus double) sets bond length and strength but does not by itself create polarity; the shared electrons in the \(\text{C}{=}\text{S}\) bond are still divided almost equally because \(\Delta\text{EN}\) remains \(\approx 0.03\). Skill 2$ex30$,
    'easy', '5B', $cc30$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc30$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a30B$assumes any bond between two different elements must be polar, ignoring the magnitude of the electronegativity difference$a30B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a30C$treats any nonzero electronegativity difference as sufficient for ionic character, misjudging the size of \(\Delta\text{EN}\) needed$a30C$ FROM q
