-- Q7 [Ionic Character & Bond Type · hard · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans C] (g31)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Ionic Character & Bond Type',
    $qt31$In solid \(\text{MgO}\), magnesium has an electronegativity of about \(1.3\) and oxygen about \(3.4\). Using the electronegativity-difference scheme that places the polar-covalent/ionic boundary near \(\Delta\text{EN} \approx 2.0\), classify the \(\text{Mg-O}\) interaction and its dominant cause.$qt31$,
    $op31$[{"label":"A","text":"Polar covalent, because \\(\\Delta\\text{EN} \\approx 2.1\\) lies in the range where shared electrons are merely unequally distributed rather than transferred"},{"label":"B","text":"Nonpolar covalent, because oxygen and magnesium both achieve a noble-gas configuration by equally sharing the bonding pair"},{"label":"C","text":"Ionic, because \\(\\Delta\\text{EN} \\approx 2.1\\) exceeds the threshold, so oxygen's far greater attraction for electrons drives essentially complete transfer to form \\(\\text{Mg}^{2+}\\) and \\(\\text{O}^{2-}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"Ionic, because magnesium's higher electronegativity pulls the bonding electrons fully onto magnesium to give \\(\\text{Mg}^{2-}\\) and \\(\\text{O}^{2+}\\)"}]$op31$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex31$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because \(\Delta\text{EN} = 3.4 - 1.3 \approx 2.1\) exceeds the \(\approx 2.0\) ionic threshold, so the bonding pair is effectively transferred rather than shared. When the electronegativity difference is this large, the more electronegative atom (oxygen) attracts the valence electrons so strongly that the bond is best modeled as full electron transfer producing discrete ions; oxygen, having the greater electronegativity, gains electrons to become the anion \(\text{O}^{2-}\) while magnesium loses its two valence electrons to become the cation \(\text{Mg}^{2+}\), consistent with the partial-ionic-character trend in which increasing \(\Delta\text{EN}\) raises ionic character. (Choice A) A \(\Delta\text{EN}\) near \(2.1\) sits above, not within, the polar-covalent window, so describing the electrons as unequally shared rather than transferred understates the degree of charge separation at this difference. (Choice B) Equal sharing requires \(\Delta\text{EN} \approx 0\); with a difference of about \(2.1\) the bonding electrons are not shared equally, so a nonpolar covalent description is inconsistent with the given values. (Choice D) The direction of charge is reversed: the more electronegative atom takes the electrons, so oxygen becomes \(\text{O}^{2-}\) and magnesium becomes \(\text{Mg}^{2+}\), not the cation/anion assignment stated. Skill 2$ex31$,
    'hard', '5B', $cc31$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc31$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
