-- Q8 [Resonance & Delocalization · medium · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans C] (g32)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Resonance & Delocalization',
    $qt32$The nitrate ion \(\text{NO}_3^-\) has a central nitrogen single-bonded to two oxygens and double-bonded to a third, with each oxygen chemically equivalent. Counting only contributors that are equivalent in energy, how many resonance structures describe \(\text{NO}_3^-\)?$qt32$,
    $op32$[{"label":"A","text":"Two equivalent resonance structures, because the negative charge alternates between the two single-bonded oxygens"},{"label":"B","text":"Four equivalent resonance structures, because three place the \\(\\text{N=O}\\) double bond and one places three \\(\\text{N=O}\\) double bonds"},{"label":"C","text":"Three equivalent resonance structures, because the \\(\\text{N=O}\\) double bond can occupy any of the three equivalent oxygen positions"},{"label":"D","text":"Six equivalent resonance structures, because each of the three oxygens can host either the double bond or the extra lone pair independently"}]$op32$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex32$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because the single \(\text{N=O}\) double bond can be drawn to any one of the three equivalent oxygens, generating exactly three resonance contributors that are identical in energy. In \(\text{NO}_3^-\) the central nitrogen forms four bonds (one double, two single) with no lone pair, and because all three oxygens are chemically equivalent, each placement of the double bond produces a structure superimposable by symmetry on the others; the delocalization spreads the charge and double-bond character equally, giving a resonance-averaged \(\text{N-O}\) bond intermediate between a single and double bond. (Choice A) Two contributors describes a species with only two equivalent terminal atoms involved in the delocalization, such as a carboxylate \(\text{RCO}_2^-\), not the three-oxygen nitrate framework. (Choice B) Counting four wrongly appends an extra "contributor" with three \(\text{N=O}\) double bonds, which would require an expanded octet on nitrogen and is not a valid Lewis structure, so it cannot be one of the equivalent contributors. (Choice D) Counting six treats the double-bond placement and a lone-pair placement as independent variations, double-counting positions that actually describe the same three structures. Skill 2$ex32$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc32$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc32$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
