-- Q9 [Resonance & Delocalization · hard · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans D] (g33)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Resonance & Delocalization',
    $qt33$The cyanate ion \(\text{OCN}^-\) (connectivity N-C-O, carbon central) has several resonance contributors that differ only in where the multiple bonds sit. Constrained to the rules of formal charge, the major (most favorable) contributor is most likely the structure with$qt33$,
    $op33$[{"label":"A","text":"a cumulated arrangement \\(\\text{N}{=}\\text{C}{=}\\text{O}\\), giving formal charges of \\(-1\\) on N, \\(0\\) on C, and \\(0\\) on O, placing the negative charge on nitrogen."},{"label":"B","text":"a single N-C bond and a C-O triple bond, \\(\\text{N}{-}\\text{C}{\\equiv}\\text{O}\\), giving formal charges of \\(-2\\) on N, \\(0\\) on C, and \\(+1\\) on O, since maximizing C-O bonding lowers the structure's energy."},{"label":"C","text":"a cumulated arrangement \\(\\text{N}{=}\\text{C}{=}\\text{O}\\) in which every atom carries a formal charge of \\(0\\), satisfying the rule that an all-zero distribution is preferred."},{"label":"D","text":"an N-C triple bond and a single C-O bond, \\(\\text{N}{\\equiv}\\text{C}{-}\\text{O}\\), giving formal charges of \\(0\\) on N, \\(0\\) on C, and \\(-1\\) on O, placing the negative charge on oxygen."}]$op33$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex33$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because the \(\text{N}{\equiv}\text{C}{-}\text{O}\) contributor minimizes the number and magnitude of nonzero formal charges and places the single \(-1\) on oxygen, the more electronegative terminal atom. Using \(\text{FC} = V - (\text{nonbonding } e^-) - \tfrac{1}{2}(\text{bonding } e^-)\): in \(\text{N}{\equiv}\text{C}{-}\text{O}\), nitrogen has one lone pair and a triple bond \((5 - 2 - 3 = 0)\), carbon has a triple plus a single bond \((4 - 0 - 4 = 0)\), and oxygen has three lone pairs and a single bond \((6 - 6 - 1 = -1)\); the charges sum to \(-1\), the ion's charge. By Guideline 2 the favorable structures are those with the smallest nonzero formal charges, leaving only \(\text{N}{\equiv}\text{C}{-}\text{O}\) (charges \(0,0,-1\)) and \(\text{N}{=}\text{C}{=}\text{O}\) (charges \(-1,0,0\)); the tiebreaker is Guideline 4, which prefers the negative formal charge on the more electronegative atom, and oxygen (\(\Delta\text{EN}\) larger) outranks nitrogen, so \(\text{N}{\equiv}\text{C}{-}\text{O}\) is the major contributor. (Choice A) This structure \(\text{N}{=}\text{C}{=}\text{O}\) does tie for the smallest formal-charge magnitude, but it locates the \(-1\) on nitrogen rather than oxygen; because oxygen is the more electronegative terminal atom, Guideline 4 makes this a minor contributor, not the major one. (Choice B) The \(\text{N}{-}\text{C}{\equiv}\text{O}\) arrangement carries a \(-2\) on nitrogen and a \(+1\) on oxygen, the largest charge separation of the set and a positive charge on the more electronegative atom; greater multiple bonding to one atom does not override the rule that smaller formal charges are preferred, so this is the least favorable structure. (Choice C) The cumulated \(\text{N}{=}\text{C}{=}\text{O}\) structure does not have all-zero formal charges; with two lone pairs and a double bond on each terminal atom the charges are \(-1\) on nitrogen and \(0\) on oxygen, so the claimed all-zero distribution misstates the formal-charge calculation. Skill 2$ex33$,
    'hard', '5B', $cc33$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc33$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
