-- Q10 [Resonance & Delocalization · medium · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans A] (g34)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Resonance & Delocalization',
    $qt34$A single Lewis structure of the carbonate ion \(\text{CO}_3^{2-}\) shows one C=O double bond and two C–O single bonds. Considering all equivalent resonance contributors, which prediction about the three carbon–oxygen bonds is correct?$qt34$,
    $op34$[{"label":"A","text":"All three bonds are identical, each shorter than a C–O single bond but longer than a C=O double bond."},{"label":"B","text":"All three bonds are identical, each equal in length to a full C=O double bond."},{"label":"C","text":"Two bonds are short like C=O double bonds and one is long like a C–O single bond."},{"label":"D","text":"All three bonds are identical, each equal in length to a full C–O single bond."}]$op34$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex34$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because resonance delocalizes the double bond equally over all three positions, so each C–O bond is an average of a single and a double bond and therefore lies intermediate in length between the two. The carbonate ion has three equivalent resonance contributors, each placing the C=O double bond on a different oxygen; the true structure is the resonance hybrid in which the extra bonding pair is shared equally, giving every bond a length and strength between those of a pure single bond and a pure double bond. (Choice B) Treating every bond as a full double bond ignores that only one double bond's worth of pi electron density is distributed across three positions, so no bond reaches full double-bond character. (Choice C) Predicting two short and one long bond corresponds to taking a single static Lewis structure literally, whereas the equivalence of the resonance contributors makes all three positions identical. (Choice D) Treating every bond as a full single bond neglects the shared pi pair entirely, which would leave the delocalized double-bond character unaccounted for. Skill 2$ex34$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc34$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc34$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
