-- Q11 [Resonance & Delocalization · medium · Skill 1 · general-chemistry · ans B] (g35)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Resonance & Delocalization',
    $qt35$The carbonate ion \(\text{CO}_3^{2-}\) is described by three equivalent resonance structures, each placing the \(\text{C=O}\) double bond on a different oxygen. Relative to any single one of these contributing structures, the actual energy of \(\text{CO}_3^{2-}\) is most likely$qt35$,
    $op35$[{"label":"A","text":"higher, because spreading the \\(\\pi\\) electrons over three \\(\\text{C-O}\\) bonds raises the energy above that of any single contributing structure"},{"label":"B","text":"lower, because delocalizing the \\(\\pi\\) electrons over all three \\(\\text{C-O}\\) bonds stabilizes the ion below any single contributing structure"},{"label":"C","text":"equal to the numerical average of the energies of the three contributing structures, since the ion spends equal time as each form"},{"label":"D","text":"equal to the energy of whichever single contributing structure is most stable, since resonance only relabels that structure"}]$op35$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex35$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is B because delocalizing the \(\pi\) electrons over all three \(\text{C-O}\) bonds spreads electron density and lowers the actual ion's energy below that of any single contributing structure. No single Lewis structure of \(\text{CO}_3^{2-}\) is real; each one localizes the \(\text{C=O}\) double bond on one oxygen, but the true ion has three equivalent \(\text{C-O}\) bonds of resonance-averaged character (between a single and a double bond) because the bonding electrons are delocalized over the whole \(\pi\) system. This delocalization is energetically favorable, so the resonance hybrid lies lower in energy (is more stable) than any individual contributing structure, and the energy difference is the resonance stabilization. (Choice A) This reverses the relationship: delocalizing electrons over more atoms lowers, rather than raises, the energy of the actual species, which is precisely why resonance is a stabilizing feature. (Choice C) The hybrid is not a time average in which the molecule flickers among the contributing forms; the contributing structures do not exist as real, interconverting species, and the actual energy is lower than the average rather than equal to it. (Choice D) Resonance is not a mere relabeling of one structure; the delocalized hybrid is genuinely lower in energy than the best single contributing structure, so equating the two ignores the resonance stabilization. Skill 1$ex35$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc35$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc35$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
