-- Q13 [Resonance & Delocalization · medium · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans D] (g37)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Resonance & Delocalization',
    $qt37$In the acetate ion \(\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-\), one resonance structure draws the carboxyl carbon double-bonded to one oxygen and single-bonded to the other (the one bearing the negative charge), while the second structure swaps those two roles. Predict how the two carbon–oxygen bond lengths in the actual ion most likely compare.$qt37$,
    $op37$[{"label":"A","text":"They differ measurably, with the \\(\\text{C=O}\\) bond shorter than the \\(\\text{C-O}^-\\) bond, because the molecule rapidly interconverts between the two structures."},{"label":"B","text":"They differ measurably, with the \\(\\text{C-O}^-\\) bond shorter, because the negative charge contracts the bond to the oxygen that carries it."},{"label":"C","text":"They are equal, each matching a full carbon–oxygen double-bond length, because every \\(\\text{C-O}\\) linkage gains a complete second bonding pair."},{"label":"D","text":"They are equal, each intermediate between a single and a double bond, because the negative charge and the extra bonding pair are delocalized equally over both oxygens."}]$op37$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex37$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because the two equivalent resonance structures contribute equally, so each carbon–oxygen bond is identical and has a length between that of a single and a double bond. Resonance means the real acetate ion is a single delocalized structure that is the weighted average of the contributors, not a molecule flipping back and forth between them. Because the two drawn structures are equivalent (they differ only in which oxygen holds the double bond and the negative charge), they contribute equally; the second bonding pair and the negative charge are spread evenly over both oxygens. Each \(\text{C-O}\) bond therefore carries about one and a half bonds' worth of bonding density, giving two equal bonds whose length lies between a pure \(\text{C-O}\) single bond and a pure \(\text{C=O}\) double bond. (Choice A) This treats resonance as a physical interconversion ("rapidly interconverts") between two distinct species, but resonance describes one averaged structure, so the bonds do not alternate in length and are not unequal. (Choice B) A localized negative charge does not shorten the bond to the oxygen carrying it; in the real ion the charge is delocalized over both oxygens, so neither \(\text{C-O}\) bond is distinguished as shorter on that basis. (Choice C) Delocalizing one extra bonding pair over two bonds gives each bond only a partial (about one-half) share of a second pair, not a full double bond apiece, so the bonds are intermediate in length rather than full double bonds. Skill 2$ex37$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc37$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc37$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
