-- Q14 [Resonance & Delocalization · hard · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans C] (g38)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Resonance & Delocalization',
    $qt38$Ozone \(\left(\text{O}_3,\ \text{connectivity O-O-O, bent}\right)\) is described by two equivalent resonance structures, in each of which one terminal O-O linkage is a double bond and the other a single bond. Treating these two structures as equal contributors, how do the two O-O bonds in the actual molecule most likely compare?$qt38$,
    $op38$[{"label":"A","text":"The two bonds differ: one is a short \\(\\text{O=O}\\) double bond and the other a longer \\(\\text{O-O}\\) single bond, as drawn in either single structure."},{"label":"B","text":"The two bonds are identical and each is a full \\(\\text{O-O}\\) single bond, because delocalizing the \\(\\pi\\) pair removes the double-bond character entirely."},{"label":"C","text":"The two bonds are identical and each is intermediate in length between a typical \\(\\text{O-O}\\) single bond and an \\(\\text{O=O}\\) double bond."},{"label":"D","text":"The two bonds are identical and each is intermediate in length between an \\(\\text{O=O}\\) double bond and an \\(\\text{O}\\equiv\\text{O}\\) triple bond."}]$op38$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex38$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because averaging two equivalent resonance structures, each with one \(\text{O=O}\) and one \(\text{O-O}\) linkage, delocalizes the single \(\pi\) pair over both positions and gives two identical bonds of order \(\approx 1.5\), intermediate between a single and a double bond. Neither resonance structure of \(\text{O}_3\) is a real, separate species; the true molecule is a single resonance hybrid in which the \(\pi\) electron pair is shared equally across both O-O regions. Because each bond therefore carries an average of one single-bond and one double-bond contribution, both bonds are equivalent and their length falls between that of a pure \(\text{O-O}\) single bond (longer, weaker) and a pure \(\text{O=O}\) double bond (shorter, stronger), consistent with the experimental observation that the two O-O distances in ozone are equal. (Choice A) Taking either individual Lewis structure literally predicts one short double bond and one long single bond, but the existence of two equivalent contributors means the hybrid has neither a localized double nor a localized single bond; the measured bond lengths are equal, not distinct. (Choice B) Delocalizing the \(\pi\) pair distributes double-bond character over both positions rather than erasing it; each bond retains roughly half of a double bond's character (order \(\approx 1.5\)), so the bonds are not pure single bonds and are shorter than a typical \(\text{O-O}\) single bond. (Choice C is keyed correct.) (Choice D) Resonance averages a single bond and a double bond to give an order of about 1.5, which lies between a single and a double bond; it does not place the bonds between a double and a triple bond, an assignment that would require double- and triple-bonded contributors that \(\text{O}_3\) does not possess. Skill 2$ex38$,
    'hard', '5B', $cc38$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc38$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
