-- Q17 [Bond Length & Bond Energy · medium · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans D] (g41)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Bond Length & Bond Energy',
    $qt41$The carbon-nitrogen bond is a single bond in methylamine \(\text{CH}_3\text{NH}_2\), a double bond in methanimine \(\text{CH}_2{=}\text{NH}\), and a triple bond in hydrogen cyanide \(\text{H}{-}\text{C}{\equiv}\text{N}\). Ranked by the energy required to break that carbon-nitrogen bond, these three molecules most likely order as$qt41$,
    $op41$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{NH}_2 > \\text{CH}_2{=}\\text{NH} > \\text{H}{-}\\text{C}{\\equiv}\\text{N}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{CH}_3\\text{NH}_2 = \\text{CH}_2{=}\\text{NH} = \\text{H}{-}\\text{C}{\\equiv}\\text{N}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{H}{-}\\text{C}{\\equiv}\\text{N} > \\text{CH}_3\\text{NH}_2 > \\text{CH}_2{=}\\text{NH}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{H}{-}\\text{C}{\\equiv}\\text{N} > \\text{CH}_2{=}\\text{NH} > \\text{CH}_3\\text{NH}_2\\)"}]$op41$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex41$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because the carbon-nitrogen triple bond in \(\text{H}{-}\text{C}{\equiv}\text{N}\) shares three electron pairs, the double bond in \(\text{CH}_2{=}\text{NH}\) shares two, and the single bond in \(\text{CH}_3\text{NH}_2\) shares one, and bond energy rises with the number of shared pairs. Each additional shared pair adds bonding electron density between the same two nuclei, so more energy must be supplied to separate the atoms; the triple bond is therefore the strongest and the single bond the weakest, giving \(\text{C}{\equiv}\text{N} > \text{C}{=}\text{N} > \text{C}{-}\text{N}\). The same trend that shortens the bond (more shared pairs pull the nuclei closer) also strengthens it, so bond length and bond energy run in opposite directions across this series. (Choice A) This ordering reverses the relationship by treating the single bond as strongest and the triple bond as weakest, but fewer shared electron pairs give a lower, not higher, bond energy. (Choice B) Equal bond energies would require the three carbon-nitrogen bonds to be identical, yet a single, double, and triple bond share different numbers of electron pairs and therefore differ in strength. (Choice C) This correctly places the triple bond as strongest but then inverts the remaining two, ranking the single bond above the double bond, whereas the double bond shares more electron pairs than the single bond and is the stronger of the two. Skill 2$ex41$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc41$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc41$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
