-- Q18 [Bond Length & Bond Energy · hard · Skill 2 · general-chemistry · ans A] (g42)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Bond Length & Bond Energy',
    $qt42$Acetylene is hydrogenated to ethylene: \(\text{C}_2\text{H}_2(g) + \text{H}_2(g) \rightarrow \text{C}_2\text{H}_4(g)\). Using only the average bond energies \(\text{C}\equiv\text{C} = 839\), \(\text{C}-\text{H} = 413\), \(\text{H}-\text{H} = 436\), and \(\text{C}=\text{C} = 614\) (all in \(\text{kJ}\,\text{mol}^{-1}\)), the estimated \(\Delta H\) for this reaction is closest to which value?$qt42$,
    $op42$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(-165\\ \\text{kJ}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+165\\ \\text{kJ}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(+225\\ \\text{kJ}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(-601\\ \\text{kJ}\\)"}]$op42$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex42$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is A because \(\Delta H = \sum(\text{bonds broken}) - \sum(\text{bonds formed}) = [839 + 2(413) + 436] - [614 + 4(413)] = 2101 - 2266 = -165\ \text{kJ}\). In the reactants, \(\text{C}_2\text{H}_2\) (connectivity \(\text{H}-\text{C}\equiv\text{C}-\text{H}\)) contributes one \(\text{C}\equiv\text{C}\) and two \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bonds, and \(\text{H}_2\) contributes one \(\text{H}-\text{H}\) bond, all of which are broken; in the product \(\text{C}_2\text{H}_4\) (connectivity \(\text{H}_2\text{C}=\text{CH}_2\)) one \(\text{C}=\text{C}\) and four \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bonds are formed, so energy released on bond formation exceeds energy absorbed on bond breaking, giving an exothermic estimate. (Choice B) reverses the sign convention by computing \(\sum(\text{bonds formed}) - \sum(\text{bonds broken})\), which yields \(+165\ \text{kJ}\) and contradicts the defined relationship \(\Delta H = \text{broken} - \text{formed}\). (Choice C) of \(+225\ \text{kJ}\) results from accounting only for the change in the carbon-carbon multiple bond, \(839 - 614\), while ignoring the \(\text{H}-\text{H}\) bond broken and the two additional \(\text{C}-\text{H}\) bonds formed. (Choice D) of \(-601\ \text{kJ}\) omits the \(\text{H}-\text{H}\) bond from the bonds-broken sum, using \([839 + 2(413)] - [614 + 4(413)]\) and thereby undercounting the energy absorbed during bond breaking. Skill 2$ex42$,
    'hard', '5B', $cc42$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc42$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a42B$Applies formed minus broken instead of broken minus formed, flipping the sign of an exothermic reaction$a42B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a42C$Considers only the changing carbon multiple bond and ignores the H-H bond broken and the new C-H bonds formed$a42C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a42D$Omits the H-H reactant bond from the bonds-broken tally, undercounting energy absorbed$a42D$ FROM q;

-- Q19 [Bond Length & Bond Energy · hard · Skill 4 · general-chemistry · ans C] (g43)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Bond Length & Bond Energy',
    $qt43$A reference table lists three carbon-carbon bonds: \(\text{C-C}\) at \(154\ \text{pm}\) and \(348\ \text{kJ/mol}\), \(\text{C=C}\) at \(134\ \text{pm}\) and \(614\ \text{kJ/mol}\), and \(\text{C} \equiv \text{C}\) at \(120\ \text{pm}\) and \(839\ \text{kJ/mol}\). Constrained to these data alone, which conclusion about the relationship among bond length, bond energy, and bond multiplicity is best supported?$qt43$,
    $op43$[{"label":"A","text":"As the number of shared electron pairs rises, the bond lengthens and the bond energy falls, so the triple bond is the longest and weakest of the three."},{"label":"B","text":"Bond energy rises with multiplicity while bond length stays essentially constant near \\(135\\ \\text{pm}\\), because added \\(\\pi\\) bonds add strength without affecting the internuclear distance."},{"label":"C","text":"As the number of shared electron pairs rises, the bond shortens and the bond energy increases, so the shortest bond \\((120\\ \\text{pm})\\) is also the strongest \\((839\\ \\text{kJ/mol})\\)."},{"label":"D","text":"Bond length and bond energy both increase together with multiplicity, so the triple bond is simultaneously the longest and the strongest of the three."}]$op43$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex43$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is C because the data show that as bond multiplicity rises from one to three shared pairs the length drops monotonically (\(154 \to 134 \to 120\ \text{pm}\)) while the energy climbs monotonically (\(348 \to 614 \to 839\ \text{kJ/mol}\)), so the shortest bond is also the strongest. Increasing the number of shared electron pairs between the same two atoms pulls the nuclei closer and requires more energy to break, which is exactly the inverse length-energy correlation the tabulated triple, double, and single carbon-carbon bonds display. (Choice A) This reverses both trends: it claims more shared pairs lengthen and weaken the bond, but the table shows the triple bond is the shortest (\(120\ \text{pm}\)) and the highest in energy (\(839\ \text{kJ/mol}\)), not the longest and weakest. (Choice B) Bond length is not constant across the series; it decreases by \(34\ \text{pm}\) from single to triple, so the data contradict the assertion that added \(\pi\) bonds leave the internuclear distance near \(135\ \text{pm}\) unchanged. (Choice C is correct.) (Choice D) Although it correctly pairs higher multiplicity with higher energy, it incorrectly couples that with increasing length; the strongest bond is the shortest, not the longest, so length and energy move in opposite directions rather than together. Skill 4$ex43$,
    'hard', '5B', $cc43$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc43$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a43A$more shared pairs read as longer and weaker bond$a43A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a43B$energy rises with multiplicity but length wrongly held constant$a43B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a43D$length and energy assumed to increase together$a43D$ FROM q;

-- Q20 [Bond Length & Bond Energy · medium · Skill 4 · general-chemistry · ans D] (g44)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Bonding & Chemical Interactions', 'Bond Length & Bond Energy',
    $qt44$A chemist tabulates the bond dissociation energies of the four hydrogen halides measured in the gas phase: \(\text{H-F}\) at \(570\ \text{kJ/mol}\), \(\text{H-Cl}\) at \(432\ \text{kJ/mol}\), \(\text{H-Br}\) at \(366\ \text{kJ/mol}\), and \(\text{H-I}\) at \(298\ \text{kJ/mol}\). Based on these data, which bond is most easily broken into its constituent atoms?$qt44$,
    $op44$[{"label":"A","text":"The \\(\\text{H-F}\\) bond"},{"label":"B","text":"The \\(\\text{H-Cl}\\) bond"},{"label":"C","text":"The \\(\\text{H-Br}\\) bond"},{"label":"D","text":"The \\(\\text{H-I}\\) bond"}]$op44$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex44$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because the bond dissociation energy is the energy required to break one mole of a bond homolytically in the gas phase, so the bond with the smallest tabulated value is the weakest and most easily broken. Among the listed values, \(\text{H-I}\) at \(298\ \text{kJ/mol}\) is the lowest, meaning the least energy must be supplied to cleave it; this reflects the trend down the halogen group, where the larger, more diffuse iodine valence orbital overlaps the hydrogen \(1s\) orbital less effectively, lengthening the bond and lowering its dissociation energy. (Choice A) The \(\text{H-F}\) value of \(570\ \text{kJ/mol}\) is the largest in the table, so this is the strongest, hardest-to-break bond rather than the weakest; selecting it reverses the relationship between bond dissociation energy and bond strength. (Choice B) The \(\text{H-Cl}\) value of \(432\ \text{kJ/mol}\) is intermediate and well above the minimum, so this bond is more difficult to break than \(\text{H-I}\). (Choice C) The \(\text{H-Br}\) value of \(366\ \text{kJ/mol}\) is lower than \(\text{H-Cl}\) but still exceeds the \(298\ \text{kJ/mol}\) of \(\text{H-I}\), so it is not the most easily broken; it is the next-weakest, a near-miss that stops one position short of the true minimum. Skill 4$ex44$,
    'medium', '5B', $cc44$Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions$cc44$, 'general-chemistry', 4, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
